[3b] E07 (landed 16:54, MID-ROUND) -- ITS GAP G1 IS ANSWERED BY THE SAME ALIGNMENT
     E07 encodes a UP2 state as A = C u {x,y}, B = C u {x+1,y+1}, and its gap G1 is:
     'the token pair can in principle re-emerge at distance >= 2', which its own endgame
     analysis says would be fatal.  MY COROLLARY TOKEN-DRIFT: at an equal-pivot step the
     two token indices i1 < i2 each descend by 1 IFF their index is <= d, and i1 > d >= i2
     is impossible, so the SIGNED gap (v1 - v2) goes to itself or to itself minus 1.
     The first version of this block asserted the ABSOLUTE gap is non-increasing.  THE RUN
     REFUTED IT (127 rises) and the corrected statement is the one that matters: the gap
     falls, stays, or rises 0 -> 1 (the signed gap going negative by one), so a gap <= 1
     CAN NEVER REACH 2.  That is exactly what E07's G1 needs.  Both readings are scored.
     TOKEN-DRIFT (corrected: gap -> gap or gap-1, and 0 -> 1) : holds 1350, FAILS 0
     distribution of the CHANGE in token gap               : {-1: 64, 0: 1159, 1: 127}
     ==> the RAW 'non-increasing' reading is FALSE: the gap DOES rise, 127 times.
     every rise starts from gap : [0]   (a rise from gap >= 1 would break the corollary)
     equal-pivot steps carrying a gap <= 1 UP TO gap >= 2  : 0   <- E07's gap G1
     E07 sec 3 claim, cross-tab (token distance, endgame outcome) : {(0, 'EQ'): 225, (0, 'OTHER'): 65, (1, 'EQ'): 281, (1, 'OTHER'): 61, (2, 'EQ'): 220, (2, 'OTHER'): 27, (3, 'EQ'): 117, (3, 'OTHER'): 4, (4, 'EQ'): 41, (5, 'EQ'): 7}
   CONTROL: the token gap actually DOES fall somewhere (drift is not trivial) hits=64       OK
